The C program is 3 functions, but all the fidelity risk sits in [38;5;10mscanf("%f", &x)[0m. I read the C, compiled a reference binary outside [38;5;10mc_src[0m (never touched [38;5;10mc_src[0m), and diffed outputs. Four glibc-specific behaviours had to be reverse-engineered rather than assumed:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mnan(...)[0m payloads are ignored by [38;5;10mscanf[0m even though [38;5;10mstrtof[0m honours them — I confirmed with a direct [38;5;10mstrtof[0m probe ([38;5;10mstrtof("nan(1)")[0m → [38;5;10m7fc00001[0m, [38;5;10mscanf[0m → [38;5;10m7fc00000[0m) and matched [38;5;10mscanf[0m.[0m[0m
- A truncated [38;5;10minfin...[0m is a total matching failure (leaves [38;5;10mx[0m at [38;5;10m0.0f[0m), not a successful [38;5;10minf[0m; [38;5;10minfinityx[0m does succeed. Committed once a 4th [38;5;10mi[0m appears.[0m[0m
- Once [38;5;10m0x[0m is consumed the scanner is committed: [38;5;10m-0x[0m and [38;5;10m-0xg[0m fail entirely ([38;5;10m00000000[0m), while [38;5;10m-0x.[0m succeeds as [38;5;10m-0.0[0m ([38;5;10m00000080[0m). The [38;5;10m-[0m sign was what exposed this, since a failure and a [38;5;10m+0.0[0m success both print [38;5;10m00000000[0m.[0m[0m
- Rust's [38;5;10mstr::parse::<f32>()[0m is not a drop-in for [38;5;10mstrtof[0m at the extremes: a 1,000,001-digit significand with [38;5;10me-999999[0m gave [38;5;10minf[0m in Rust vs [38;5;10m31.111[0m in C. I now canonicalize the token (strip zeros, truncate to 800 significant digits with a sticky digit, clamp the exponent) before parsing, which keeps rounding exact and is verified against C on multi-megabyte inputs.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Hex floats are parsed by hand (u128 accumulation, sticky bit, round-to-nearest-even) since Rust has no hex-float parser; [38;5;10mdriver[0m uses [38;5;10mto_ne_bytes()[0m to mirror the [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m of the object representation.[0m[0m
